          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use signature",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "on elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "post-qft-free-photon-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the following specified free-photon model in the oriented,\ntime-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and length coordinates of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], with metric $(-,+,+,+)$ and $x^0=ct$. The\nclassical electromagnetic input is the smooth source-free branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]; the electromagnetic two-form and its potential\ncomponents are those of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], and a supplied\npotential is subject to [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. These EM suppliers\nare physical input and conventions: they do not by themselves construct a\nquantum representation.\n\nFor almost every $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, set\n$\\mathbf n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T}$. The mathematical\ndefinition [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]] specifies the positive\none-photon Hilbert space\n\n$$\\mathcal H_T= \\{u\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3): \\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\},$$\n\nwith the inherited pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k$,\nlinear in the second variable as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The\nvalue assigned to $P_T$ at\n$\\mathbf k=0$ is immaterial because that point has Lebesgue measure zero; no\ncontinuous polarization frame over all momentum directions is chosen. Adopt\nthe bosonic Fock representation $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$ and the free\nHamiltonian\n\n$$H_\\gamma=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|),$$\n\nwhere on the $n$-photon sector $d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|)$ is multiplication by\n$\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$ and is zero on the vacuum sector. Its exact\nself-adjoint domain and the unitary mass-cone action are those in the\nmathematical supplier [[lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum]]. The actual\nsmeared field-strength distributions and bounded Weyl operators are those\nconstructed in [[lem-qft-photon-local-weyl-observables]]; a pointwise\npotential or field-strength operator is not asserted to exist. These\nmathematical suppliers are draft carriers with mixed current Step 1 status: the\ntransverse-space, null-quotient, covariance-spectrum, framework-units, and\nC01 measurement carriers have current ready records; the local-Weyl carrier is\nescalated through its field-strength prerequisite. Readiness records are not\nindependent approval, and every use remains conditional on the exact statement\nand proof supplied.\n\nFor the physical interpretation of an actually constructed state and a\nsupplied normalized measurement instrument, use\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]: Born probabilities are\nassigned only to that specified model and instrument. This postulate records\nthe model's free Maxwell quanta as photons. It does not assert that the\nmodel has been empirically confirmed, and it introduces no interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space, scattering operator, LSZ limit,\nasymptotic completeness, or nonperturbative continuum limit. In calculations\nusing an indefinite four-polarization auxiliary space, only the positive\ntransverse model or its separately constructed null quotient is physical;\nfour auxiliary components are not four positive photon states.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The energy is the nonnegative sector multiplier",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-coherent-ir-obstruction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the transverse one-particle space $\\mathcal H_T$ and multiplier $\\omega(\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier]], and form its symmetric Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$. Let $H_{\\rm ph}:=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(\\omega)$, with $\\hbar,c>0$ and $d\\Gamma(\\omega)$ acting on the $n$-particle sector as multiplication by $\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\mathbf k_j|$, as in [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]. For a coherent vector use\n$$ \\epsilon(f):=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f^{\\otimes n}}{\\sqrt{n!}},\\qquad C(f):=e^{-\\|f\\|^2/2}\\epsilon(f), $$\nusing the tensor and direct-sum conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]].\n\nLet $\\kappa>0$. Let $b:S^2\\to\\mathbb C^3$ be measurable with $b\\in L^2(S^2,d\\Omega;\\mathbb C^3)$, $\\mathbf n\\cdot b(\\mathbf n)=0$ for a.e. $\\mathbf n$, and\n$$ C_b:=\\int_{S^2}|b(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Omega>0. $$\nTake $\\mathbf k,\\kappa,\\epsilon$ in inverse metres and $b$ dimensionless; then $f_\\epsilon$ has momentum-wavefunction units $\\mathrm m^{3/2}$, $C_b$ is dimensionless, and $\\hbar c(\\kappa-\\epsilon)$ has energy units.\nFor $0<\\epsilon<\\kappa$, define $f_\\epsilon(0)=0$ and\n$$ f_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k):=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\epsilon<|\\mathbf k|<\\kappa\\}}|\\mathbf k|^{-3/2}b\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf k}{|\\mathbf k|}\\right). $$\nThen\n$$ \\|f_\\epsilon\\|^2=C_b\\log\\frac{\\kappa}{\\epsilon},\\qquad \\langle C(f_\\epsilon),H_{\\rm ph}C(f_\\epsilon)\\rangle =\\hbar c\\,C_b(\\kappa-\\epsilon). $$\nEach $C(f_\\epsilon)$ is a unit vector in $D(H_{\\rm ph})$. As $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, $C(f_\\epsilon)\\rightharpoonup0$ weakly, but the family has no strong Fock-space limit. The statement is an exact free transverse-mode construction; it asserts no interacting charged sector, continuum QED limit, or empirical observation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]. Let S be a compact embedded $C^1$ hypersurface in $\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$. Choose finitely many regular injective $C^1$ parametrizations $X_j:V_j\\to S\\cap O_j$ with $C^1$ coordinate transitions, and an ambient partition $\\chi_j$ from [[lem-finite-ambient-partitions-for-euclidean-boundary-integration]], with supports compactly contained in the corresponding chart neighborhoods. Put $J_{X_j}=\\sqrt{\\det(DX_j^TDX_j)}$, using [[def-gram-matrix-and-gram-determinant]].\n\nFor a nonnegative Borel f on S define $\\int_S f\\,dS=\\sum_j\\int_{V_j}(\\chi_j f)(X_j(y))J_{X_j}(y)\\,dy$, where the integrals on the right are those of [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]] and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Set $S(A)=\\int_S\\mathbf1_A\\,dS$ for Borel A. For signed f with $\\int_S|f|\\,dS<\\infty$ use the difference of the positive and negative integrals. The same chart formula restricts to a compact Borel face contained in a regular patch. The empty surface has zero integral. Independence and finiteness are established by the following chart-independence lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let\n$\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a\nfinite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put\n$H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric\nsubspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating\n[[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$,\n$s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set\n$$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate\n$s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain\n$$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nEach $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a\ngraph core. The joint projection-valued measure\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$\nby $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then\n$$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm\nderivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is\n$iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint\nvalue $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then:\n\n1. **Degenerate boxes.** If $a_i \\le b_i$ are reals with $a_{i_0} = b_{i_0}$ for\n   some $i_0 < n$, then every set $R$ between the open box\n   $\\{x : a_i < x_i < b_i\\ (i<n)\\}$ and the closed rectangle $[a,b]$ is Lebesgue\n   measurable with $\\lambda_n(R) = 0$.\n2. **Coordinate hyperplanes.** For $i_0 < n$ and a real $c$, the set\n   $$H_{i_0,c} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : x_{i_0} = c \\,\\}$$\n   is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(H_{i_0,c}) = 0$.\n\nAt $n = 1$ the hyperplane $H_{0,c}$ is the singleton $\\{c\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-qed-infrared-sequence-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $\\mathbf n\\in S^2$, set\n$b(\\mathbf n)=\\lambda(I_3-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^T)\\mathbf e$. Then\n$\\mathbf n\\cdot b(\\mathbf n)=0$. Since $\\mathbf e$ is unit,\n$|b(\\mathbf n)|^2=\\lambda^2(1-(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf e)^2)$. The function\nis continuous on $S^2$ and hence measurable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-qed-infrared-sequence-step-2-1",
          "claim": "By the orthogonal invariance in [F4], take $\\mathbf e$ as the third coordinate axis and put $u=\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf e$. The chart $X(u,\\phi)=(\\sqrt{1-u^2}\\cos\\phi,\\sqrt{1-u^2}\\sin\\phi,u)$ for $-1<u<1$, $0<\\phi<2\\pi$ has orthogonal tangent vectors with squared lengths $(1-u^2)^{-1}$ and $1-u^2$, so [F5] gives area density $1$. The omitted seam is a coordinate line in sphere graph charts and the poles are singletons; [F7] makes their chart integrals zero. Thus spherical integration gives\n$$C_b=\\int_{S^2}|b(\\mathbf n)|^2d\\Omega =2\\pi\\lambda^2\\int_{-1}^1(1-u^2)\\,du =\\frac{8\\pi\\lambda^2}{3}>0.$$\nUsing $d^3\\mathbf k=r^2dr\\,d\\Omega$ and\n$|f_\\epsilon(r\\mathbf n)|^2=r^{-3}|b(\\mathbf n)|^2$ gives\n$$\\|f_\\epsilon\\|^2=C_b\\int_\\epsilon^\\kappa\\frac{dr}{r} =C_b\\log(\\kappa/\\epsilon),\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\hbar c|\\mathbf k||f_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)|^2d^3\\mathbf k =\\hbar c C_b\\int_\\epsilon^\\kappa dr =\\hbar c C_b(\\kappa-\\epsilon).$$\nBoth integrals are finite because $0<\\epsilon<\\kappa$. The profile has units\n$\\mathrm m^{3/2}$ because $|\\mathbf k|^{-3/2}$ has those units and $b$ is\ndimensionless; the energy integral has units joules because $\\hbar c$ has\nunits joule-metres. Thus $b$ satisfies the nonzero transverse-profile\nhypothesis of [L2] with the conventions [F1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "L2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-qed-infrared-sequence-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The definition of $b$ gives\n$f_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf1_{\\epsilon<|\\mathbf k|<\\kappa}\n|\\mathbf k|^{-3/2}b(\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|)$. Applying [L2] with\n$C_b=8\\pi\\lambda^2/3$ and the Hamiltonian of [L1] gives the normalized\ncoherent vector, its energy domain membership, both expectation\nformulas, weak convergence to zero, and failure of strong Fock-space\nconvergence. For the number domain, put $r=\\|f_\\epsilon\\|^2$. By [F3],\nits squared sector norms are $e^{-r}r^n/n!$, so reindexing the absolutely\nconvergent exponential series gives\n$\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2e^{-r}r^n/n!=r^2+r<\\infty$. The maximal number\nmultiplier from [F6] therefore contains this vector in its domain. This\nrestriction to the transverse space preserves the domain because each\nsector multiplier $nI$ preserves every transverse symmetric sector. The item-level suppliers are present in the active manifests,\nbut [L1] has no Step-1 record and [L2] is escalated, so this application\nremains conditional rather than accepted.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-qed-infrared-sequence-step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\epsilon_j=\\kappa 2^{-j}$ for $j\\ge1$. By step 3.1 the difference\n$f_{\\epsilon_{j+1}}-f_{\\epsilon_j}$ is supported on\n$\\epsilon_{j+1}<|\\mathbf k|<\\epsilon_j$, so spherical integration gives\n$$\\|f_{\\epsilon_{j+1}}-f_{\\epsilon_j}\\|^2 =C_b\\int_{\\epsilon_{j+1}}^{\\epsilon_j}\\frac{dr}{r} =C_b\\log 2>0.$$\nFrom the Fock direct-sum pairing in [F3], the tensor product pairing on\nsector $n$ gives\n$$\\langle C(f),C(g)\\rangle =e^{-(\\|f\\|^2+\\|g\\|^2)/2} \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\langle f,g\\rangle^n}{n!} =e^{-(\\|f\\|^2+\\|g\\|^2)/2+\\langle f,g\\rangle},$$\nwhere the series is absolutely convergent by\n$|\\langle f,g\\rangle|\\le\\|f\\|\\|g\\|$. Hence\n $|\\langle C(f),C(g)\\rangle|=e^{-\\|f-g\\|^2/2}$. By [L2],\n$\\|C(f_{\\epsilon_j})\\|=1$ for each $j$. For any choices of phases\n$\\alpha_j\\in\\mathbb R$, the unit vectors\n$v_j=e^{i\\alpha_j}C(f_{\\epsilon_j})$ satisfy\n$$\\|v_{j+1}-v_j\\|^2 =2-2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle v_j,v_{j+1}\\rangle \\ge2(1-e^{-C_b\\log 2/2})>0.$$\nThis bound holds for every phase sequence, so none is Cauchy. Any\nphase-adjusted norm limit as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ would restrict to a\nCauchy sequence along $\\epsilon_j$; therefore no such limit exists.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "L2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-qed-infrared-sequence-step-5-1",
          "claim": "By [L2], each vector has norm one and the weak limit is zero. Since the\nzero vector has norm zero, it is not a normalized state; the nonexistence of\na norm limit follows from steps 3.1 and 4.1.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes 0<epsilon<kappa, so it makes no claim for an empty cutoff band."
